theory.intro:
The gradient of a curve at a point $P(c, f(c))$ is the gradient of the tangent there. To estimate it, pick a nearby point $Q(c+h, f(c+h))$ and find the gradient of the secant $PQ$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=0.2:4.2, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.25*\x*\x+0.5}) node[pos=0.97, above left] {$y=f(x)$};
\coordinate (P) at (1.6,1.14);
\coordinate (Q) at (3.6,3.74);
\coordinate (R) at (3.6,1.14);
\draw[dashed] (1.6,0) -- (P);
\draw[dashed] (0,1.14) -- (P);
\draw[dashed] (3.6,0) -- (R);
\draw[dashed] (0,3.74) -- (Q);
\coordinate (S1) at (0.9,0.23);
\coordinate (S2) at (4.3,4.65);
\draw (S1) -- (S2) node[right, font=\normalsize] {secant};
\draw (P) -- (R) node[midway, below] {$h$};
\draw (R) -- (Q) node[midway, right, font=\normalsize] {$f(c+h)-f(c)$};
\fill (P) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$P$};
\fill (Q) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$Q$};
\node[below] at (1.6,-0.05) {$c$};
\node[below] at (3.6,-0.05) {$c+h$};
\node[left] at (0,1.14) {$f(c)$};
\node[left] at (0,3.74) {$f(c+h)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
